ct's buried stretches (where the terrain stands above its gradient line) are the base's line on ground part 0, never an underground system. §2.3a gains a row: aqueduct_or_water_system makes no system, V11's conduit_house binding stands, and the terminal cistern is its underground part. (C4-110-7, major, buildability: item 110 (a) reaching U-NET) A way crossing a RetainingWall steps its ground within a few metres, but N7 samples heights only at spine vertices, so an invert interpolated between two terraces can breach the lower ground. N6 adds two knots on every followed way at each wall crossing, at the high and low faces, with heights from the levelled ground that the base's S10/S11 publish. Where minGradient cannot keep coverMinCm, N7 takes a vertical drop at the wall (a drop junction drawn as a shaft). §3.5.4's street vaults measure grade over the ramped profile only, without the stair intervals, so a stair at a wall is not a vault source. Bars: 0 crowns above the levelled ground, and with the terrace rows off U-NET is byte-identical. (C4-110-8, blocker, item 110 (c) and the 23:22 note's (5)) Ruins have no stepped monuments. V4 §3.5.0a (5) gains a monument rule. Where the culture row's monument cell (V11 X17) names V02's stepped-monument row, the temple chamber holds a monument, and so does the central hall at significance palace, and the necropolis's largest tomb where the row flags it. layTiers lays it: frame the chamber's rectangle on axisDeg (u along the axis, origin at the front corner toward the approach), region that rectangle less monumentClearM (3 m) on each side, scale 1, the row's TierCells, draws on sewer keyed by (the ruin system, the plan coordinate, 'tiers'). The chamber's ceiling rises to the laid height + 200 cm, capped at burialCm - 100, and tiers above the cap are truncated. Step 6 decays the tiers from the top: tier k stands while its uniform falls below survival[hall]^(age/ref) x tierDecay^k (0.85, our own), and the first failing tier and every tier above it become a rubble mound. The result is stored as Chamber.monument {lay: TierLay, standing, truncated}: persisted, re-laid with the chamber, re-laid through layTiers on a setAttr of the tier count, and laid at the next ruin derivation when absent. U-CITY treats the tiers as obstacles, and the ruin program draws one ring and one shadow per standing tier under the light rows. Exports: GeoJSON rings and glTF prisms. The terraced family's terrace edges take the retaining-wall mark. Bars: tiers nest, standing <= laid, the top stays under the ceiling, and with the cell off the ruin is byte-identical. (C4-110-9, major, tests: item 110 (f)) New bars T-V4-62 to T-V4-70. They cover: arch vaults only in 'vault' spans, none over a way, water or a flood cell, with headroom >= the minimum; a removed generated raised way leaves no vaults after regeneration, and undo restores the ids; no sewer edge, junction or grating on a raised part; the support and footing reach; the aqueduct seam, including a base verdict identical whether or not V04's tail has run; retaining-wall crowns and drops; monuments; the no-op pin (terrace, raised-way, deck-plot and monument rows off: V04 byte-identical to V04 before item 110); laziness (districts opened in two orders give the same arch-vault set). (C4-110-10, minor, rendering) Arch vaults are drawn in the shallowest band with the vault hatch, and raised parts appear in the cut-away as outlines only. The ground view draws nothing of V04, since the base draws the span's infill and door. The door is a daylight opening, as §8.4's shafts are. The vaults naming slice names the run of a way's arch vaults after the way. GeoJSON gives an absolute floor and ceiling, VTT takes its walls from the support faces with the door as a portal, and glTF exports the interior only. (C4-110-11, minor, performance) The T2 row in §6 ('vaults <= 60 chambers') does not count arch vaults. Add: arch vaults <= the base's cap (proposed 240 at METROPOLIS), O(spans), <= 3 ms p95; monuments <= 2 ms each. (C4-110-12, minor, wiring and UX) §1b gains rows for arch vaults, Chamber.monument, the support and footing obstacles, the aqueduct's delivery points, and aqueduct_or_water_system -> conduit_house -> terminal cistern. V10 gains why-providers: the span's reason, the aqueduct serving a precinct, a support's footing in a cave. Proposed decisions J-V4.79 to J-V4.84: the span use is an eager base fact; the arch vault is an at-grade chamber in the vaults system rather than a ground building; arch vaults are not undercity voids and do not fade; supports are foundations that fill the voids they meet; the aqueduct's buried stretches are not an underground system; monuments go through layTiers and decay from the top. The BCRs are II.7.6's Vaults bullet (the arch source) and §2.7's chamber kind. §8's U3 gains about 1.25 to 2 pw, depending on the base's raised-way record and V02's layTiers (both M3a). Still uncarried, as the 23:22 note says: the hand-offs from revise-V7 (22:42) and revise-V3 (22:48). 
